# Strategy

Family of algorithms, same interface, pick one at runtime. Open/closed: add a class, not another `if`.

## Anatomy

- Strategy interface.
- Concrete strategies as `@Injectable()`.
- Context holds a map / default token and delegates.

```ts
export interface PricingStrategy {
  readonly name: string;
  calculate(basePrice: number, quantity: number): PricingResult;
}

export const PRICING_STRATEGY = Symbol('PRICING_STRATEGY');

@Injectable()
export class PricingService {
  constructor(
    @Inject(PRICING_STRATEGY) private readonly defaultStrategy: PricingStrategy,
    @Inject(ALL_PRICING_STRATEGIES) strategies: PricingStrategy[],
  ) {
    this.strategies = new Map(strategies.map((s) => [s.name, s]));
  }

  calculatePrice(basePrice: number, quantity: number, name?: string) {
    const strategy = name ? this.strategies.get(name) : this.defaultStrategy;
    if (!strategy) throw AppError.badRequest(`Unknown strategy: ${name}`);
    return strategy.calculate(basePrice, quantity);
  }
}
```

Module: `useExisting` for the default token; `useFactory` to gather `ALL_*`.

## Nest mapping

`TusHandlerRegistry` is Strategy-shaped: `UploadType` → `TusUploadHandler`. Feature modules `register` at startup; the `/files` endpoint does not `switch` on upload type.

```ts
@Injectable()
export class TusHandlerRegistry {
  private readonly handlers = new Map<UploadType, TusUploadHandler>();

  register(uploadType: UploadType, handler: TusUploadHandler) {
    this.handlers.set(uploadType, handler);
  }

  get(uploadType: UploadType) {
    return this.handlers.get(uploadType);
  }
}
```

Injection tokens live in `tokens/` (`TUS_OPTIONS`).

State vs Strategy: State transitions itself; Strategy is chosen by the caller and does not morph into another strategy as a lifecycle.

## When to use

- Several algorithms (pricing, hashing, upload handlers).
- Eliminate `switch` on type for behavior.

## When not to use

- One algorithm.
- Strategies need huge shared mutable context — keep it a workflow.

